WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.345
Lesson zero point three.

00:00:02.345 --> 00:00:04.990
Buying your first crypto without overpaying.

00:00:04.990 --> 00:00:07.260
By the end, you'll fund your account and

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:09.530
make a first purchase at a sensible cost.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:13.165
Here's why this lesson earns its place.

00:00:13.165 --> 00:00:15.602
On the same exchange, buying the exact

00:00:15.602 --> 00:00:17.852
same coin, the button you press can

00:00:17.852 --> 00:00:20.340
change your cost by ten times over.

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.614
Nobody warns you about this, because both buttons say

00:00:23.614 --> 00:00:26.645
“buy.” One of them is just quietly more expensive.

00:00:27.945 --> 00:00:29.235
Here's the plan.

00:00:29.235 --> 00:00:31.900
First, how to get money onto the exchange.

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.051
Second, why the instant-buy button often costs more,

00:00:35.051 --> 00:00:38.145
and the trading screen that usually costs less.

00:00:38.145 --> 00:00:40.680
Third, what to buy first for this program.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.144
And finally, a real worked example with

00:00:43.144 --> 00:00:45.608
real numbers, your checklist and a quiz.

00:00:46.908 --> 00:00:47.923
Getting money in.

00:00:49.223 --> 00:00:51.158
First, deposit your money.

00:00:51.158 --> 00:00:53.544
A bank transfer is usually the cheapest

00:00:53.544 --> 00:00:55.095
way in, but it can take a

00:00:55.095 --> 00:00:56.708
day or more to arrive.

00:00:56.708 --> 00:00:59.566
A card is fast, often instant, but it usually

00:00:59.566 --> 00:01:02.725
carries a much higher fee for the convenience.

00:01:02.725 --> 00:01:04.060
Neither is wrong.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.640
Pick based on whether you need the money working

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.905
today, or you're happy to plan a day ahead.

00:01:10.205 --> 00:01:13.360
Here's the whole sequence, deposit to first buy.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:16.750
You deposit, by whichever method fits your timeline.

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:19.408
You wait for funds to clear, instantly for a

00:01:19.408 --> 00:01:22.070
card, a day or more for a bank transfer.

00:01:22.070 --> 00:01:23.845
You choose your route to buy.

00:01:23.845 --> 00:01:26.700
And only then do you actually place the order.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.742
Skipping ahead doesn't save time, the

00:01:28.742 --> 00:01:30.355
funds simply aren't there yet.

00:01:31.655 --> 00:01:33.717
Why does a bank transfer take a day

00:01:33.717 --> 00:01:35.970
or more, when a card is instant?

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:39.212
It's simply going through a slower system: your bank

00:01:39.212 --> 00:01:42.882
and the exchange's bank settle it between themselves, often

00:01:42.882 --> 00:01:46.240
only on business days, sometimes queued overnight.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:49.877
A card authorises instantly because the card network fronts

00:01:49.877 --> 00:01:53.150
the money and settles later, behind the scenes, which

00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:56.120
is exactly what you're paying its higher fee for.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:58.865
Buying without overpaying.

00:02:00.165 --> 00:02:02.508
Here's exactly how an instant-buy or

00:02:02.508 --> 00:02:05.215
convert button quietly costs you more.

00:02:05.215 --> 00:02:06.420
You tap buy.

00:02:06.420 --> 00:02:09.299
The exchange quotes you a price that's already marked

00:02:09.299 --> 00:02:12.265
up above the real market price, called the spread.

00:02:12.265 --> 00:02:14.775
It adds a convenience fee on top of that.

00:02:14.775 --> 00:02:17.018
And you receive your crypto, having paid

00:02:17.018 --> 00:02:19.315
both, without ever seeing them broken out.

00:02:20.615 --> 00:02:24.086
The alternative is the trading screen, sometimes called

00:02:24.086 --> 00:02:26.999
“advanced” or “spot trading.” It uses an order

00:02:26.999 --> 00:02:29.530
book, and gives you two order types.

00:02:29.530 --> 00:02:31.583
A market order buys right now, at

00:02:31.583 --> 00:02:34.118
whatever the best available price is.

00:02:34.118 --> 00:02:36.422
A limit order buys only at your chosen

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.785
price or better, and it often carries a

00:02:38.785 --> 00:02:41.267
lower “maker” fee for adding to the order

00:02:41.267 --> 00:02:43.098
book instead of taking from it.

00:02:44.398 --> 00:02:46.913
So when do you actually reach for each one?

00:02:46.913 --> 00:02:49.834
Use a market order when speed matters more than

00:02:49.834 --> 00:02:52.754
the price, a small, one-off purchase, or a coin

00:02:52.754 --> 00:02:56.218
trading actively enough that the price barely moves.

00:02:56.218 --> 00:02:58.450
Use a limit order whenever the price

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:00.682
matters more than speed, which for a

00:03:00.682 --> 00:03:02.913
first purchase is nearly always.

00:03:02.913 --> 00:03:04.806
You're not in a rush, so let the lower

00:03:04.806 --> 00:03:06.698
fee, and the better price, come to you.

00:03:07.998 --> 00:03:10.873
Here's placing a limit order, step by step.

00:03:10.873 --> 00:03:12.581
Open the trading or advanced

00:03:12.581 --> 00:03:14.943
screen, not the instant-buy button.

00:03:14.943 --> 00:03:17.533
Select limit order, not market order.

00:03:17.533 --> 00:03:19.538
Enter the price you're willing to pay.

00:03:19.538 --> 00:03:21.263
Enter the amount you want.

00:03:21.263 --> 00:03:22.323
Submit it.

00:03:22.323 --> 00:03:24.323
And then wait, because it only fills

00:03:24.323 --> 00:03:26.377
if the market reaches your price, and

00:03:26.377 --> 00:03:27.728
it might not fill at all.

00:03:29.028 --> 00:03:30.118
Quick check.

00:03:30.118 --> 00:03:32.461
Which order type actually controls the

00:03:32.461 --> 00:03:34.383
price you pay: market, or limit?

00:03:38.803 --> 00:03:40.728
The answer: a limit order.

00:03:40.728 --> 00:03:42.695
A market order accepts whatever

00:03:42.695 --> 00:03:44.478
price is available right now.

00:03:45.778 --> 00:03:47.810
One note of balance: instant buy

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:49.958
isn't always the wrong choice.

00:03:49.958 --> 00:03:52.519
For a genuinely tiny amount, or when you'd

00:03:52.519 --> 00:03:55.497
rather spend thirty seconds than ten minutes, the

00:03:55.497 --> 00:03:58.180
extra cost can be worth the convenience.

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:00.526
The point of this lesson isn't “never use

00:04:00.526 --> 00:04:03.320
it.” It's knowing the trade-off exists, so you're

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:05.275
choosing it, not stumbling into it.

00:04:06.576 --> 00:04:08.584
Whichever route you take, check your

00:04:08.584 --> 00:04:10.621
confirmation before you move on.

00:04:10.621 --> 00:04:13.231
It shows the price you actually paid, the fee

00:04:13.231 --> 00:04:16.481
charged, and the amount that landed in your account.

00:04:16.481 --> 00:04:19.301
Compare that amount against what you expected.

00:04:19.301 --> 00:04:21.861
If it's meaningfully different, that gap is

00:04:21.861 --> 00:04:23.665
exactly what a spread or a fee

00:04:23.665 --> 00:04:25.061
looks like in real life.

00:04:26.361 --> 00:04:27.456
What to buy first.

00:04:28.756 --> 00:04:31.001
For this program, buy two things.

00:04:31.001 --> 00:04:33.305
A little ETH, because you need it

00:04:33.305 --> 00:04:35.746
to pay network fees, called gas, on

00:04:35.746 --> 00:04:38.960
Ethereum and most of its low-cost networks.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:40.809
And some USDC, a dollar

00:04:40.809 --> 00:04:43.805
stablecoin, for practice with DeFi.

00:04:43.805 --> 00:04:46.210
Neither is a prediction about price.

00:04:46.210 --> 00:04:49.075
They're the two tools this program actually uses.

00:04:50.374 --> 00:04:53.364
Here's why the ETH part isn't optional, even

00:04:53.364 --> 00:04:56.389
if stablecoins are all you actually want.

00:04:56.389 --> 00:04:58.916
Hold only USDC, and try your first

00:04:58.916 --> 00:05:02.382
DeFi transaction, and it fails: there's nothing

00:05:02.382 --> 00:05:04.704
to pay the network fee with.

00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:07.135
Hold a little ETH alongside it, and

00:05:07.135 --> 00:05:09.769
the same transaction just goes through.

00:05:11.069 --> 00:05:12.514
How much of each?

00:05:12.514 --> 00:05:15.251
There's no fixed rule, and it depends on

00:05:15.251 --> 00:05:18.554
your overall learning budget from Lesson 0.0.

00:05:18.554 --> 00:05:22.371
A common approach is enough ETH to cover several

00:05:22.371 --> 00:05:26.578
transactions' worth of gas, often just a few dollars,

00:05:26.578 --> 00:05:29.604
with the rest in USDC for practice.

00:05:29.604 --> 00:05:31.797
You can always buy a little more of either,

00:05:31.797 --> 00:05:34.239
later, once you see how you're actually using it.

00:05:35.539 --> 00:05:36.944
A real worked example.

00:05:38.244 --> 00:05:39.907
Here's the shape of it, in one

00:05:39.907 --> 00:05:42.085
picture, before we run the numbers.

00:05:42.085 --> 00:05:44.667
Every purchase has a real cost hiding in

00:05:44.667 --> 00:05:47.045
it: the spread, the fee, or both.

00:05:47.045 --> 00:05:49.180
The route you choose decides how much

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:50.865
of that cost you actually pay.

00:05:52.166 --> 00:05:56.794
Here are two real routes to the same $500

00:05:56.794 --> 00:06:01.862
of ETH. Card plus instant buy: a typical cost

00:06:01.862 --> 00:06:06.349
of about 3.99%, around $19.95 in fees.

00:06:06.349 --> 00:06:10.317
Bank transfer plus a limit order: a typical

00:06:10.317 --> 00:06:14.044
cost of about 0.4%, around $2 in fees.

00:06:14.044 --> 00:06:17.992
Same coin, same exchange, about an $18 difference.

00:06:17.992 --> 00:06:21.248
Your own exchange's fee page has the exact numbers,

00:06:21.248 --> 00:06:23.377
but the shape of this gap is real.

00:06:24.677 --> 00:06:28.019
Put another way: about $480.05 of

00:06:28.019 --> 00:06:31.267
ETH arrives the expensive way.

00:06:31.267 --> 00:06:34.937
About $498 arrives the cheaper way.

00:06:34.937 --> 00:06:36.912
Same $500 in.

00:06:36.912 --> 00:06:39.327
A real difference in what actually shows up.

00:06:40.627 --> 00:06:43.042
And that gap isn't a one-time cost.

00:06:43.042 --> 00:06:44.835
It repeats every single time

00:06:44.835 --> 00:06:46.742
you buy the expensive way.

00:06:46.742 --> 00:06:48.895
Learn the cheaper route once, on this

00:06:48.895 --> 00:06:50.991
first small purchase, and it can pay

00:06:50.991 --> 00:06:52.747
off on every purchase after it.

00:06:54.047 --> 00:06:56.999
And the percentages hold as

00:06:56.999 --> 00:06:59.498
the amount grows. The same

00:06:59.498 --> 00:07:02.574
roughly 3.99% versus 0.4% gap,

00:07:02.574 --> 00:07:07.644
applied to an illustrative $5,000 purchase instead of $500,

00:07:07.644 --> 00:07:11.933
is about $199 against about $20. Same habit.

00:07:11.933 --> 00:07:13.133
A bigger

00:07:13.133 --> 00:07:14.418
number, later.

00:07:15.718 --> 00:07:16.628
Keep a record.

00:07:17.928 --> 00:07:20.154
Save a record of every purchase, from

00:07:20.154 --> 00:07:22.204
day one: the date, the amount, the

00:07:22.204 --> 00:07:24.203
price, and the fees you paid.

00:07:24.203 --> 00:07:27.043
Most countries tax crypto gains, and this simple

00:07:27.043 --> 00:07:30.288
habit is what makes that straightforward later, instead

00:07:30.288 --> 00:07:33.128
of a scramble through months of exchange history.

00:07:34.428 --> 00:07:36.708
It's not only for tax season, either.

00:07:36.708 --> 00:07:39.174
That same record is the only honest way

00:07:39.174 --> 00:07:42.010
to know what you actually paid for something,

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:44.908
months or years later, once memory has quietly

00:07:44.908 --> 00:07:47.058
rounded the number up or down.

00:07:47.058 --> 00:07:49.172
A spreadsheet, a notes app, even a

00:07:49.172 --> 00:07:52.008
photo of the confirmation screen all work.

00:07:52.008 --> 00:07:53.843
The habit matters more than the tool.

00:07:55.143 --> 00:07:57.038
Here's this lesson's checklist.

00:07:57.038 --> 00:08:00.303
Deposited by the cheapest method available to you.

00:08:00.303 --> 00:08:02.727
Bought a small amount of ETH,

00:08:02.727 --> 00:08:05.613
for gas, and USDC, for practice.

00:08:05.613 --> 00:08:08.038
Tried a limit order on the trading screen.

00:08:08.038 --> 00:08:11.372
And saved a record: date, amount, price and fees.

00:08:12.672 --> 00:08:13.917
Question two. Why

00:08:13.917 --> 00:08:15.592
keep a little ETH even if

00:08:15.592 --> 00:08:18.169
you mostly want stablecoins? [[pause 4]]

00:08:22.589 --> 00:08:24.706
The answer: you need ETH to pay

00:08:24.706 --> 00:08:27.352
network fees, gas, on Ethereum and most

00:08:27.352 --> 00:08:30.099
of its low-cost networks. Without it,

00:08:30.099 --> 00:08:32.794
a transaction simply can't go through.

00:08:34.094 --> 00:08:35.364
Question three.

00:08:35.364 --> 00:08:37.029
Why keep records from day one?

00:08:41.449 --> 00:08:44.694
The answer: most countries tax crypto gains, and a

00:08:44.694 --> 00:08:48.193
simple record of dates, amounts, prices and fees makes

00:08:48.193 --> 00:08:51.374
that straightforward, instead of a scramble later.

00:08:52.674 --> 00:08:53.929
Question four.

00:08:53.929 --> 00:08:56.786
True or false: the instant-buy button and the

00:08:56.786 --> 00:08:59.519
trading screen always cost roughly the same.

00:09:03.819 --> 00:09:05.454
The answer: false.

00:09:05.454 --> 00:09:08.401
This lesson's own worked example showed close

00:09:08.401 --> 00:09:10.964
to a ten-times difference, on the exact

00:09:10.964 --> 00:09:13.399
same coin, on the exact same exchange.

00:09:14.699 --> 00:09:17.509
Here's the whole lesson, recapped as one loop.

00:09:17.509 --> 00:09:19.130
You deposit, by the cheapest

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:20.884
method that works for you.

00:09:20.884 --> 00:09:22.742
You choose the trading screen over

00:09:22.742 --> 00:09:24.369
instant buy when you can.

00:09:24.369 --> 00:09:25.944
You place a limit order.

00:09:25.944 --> 00:09:28.112
You buy a little E.T.H. and some

00:09:28.112 --> 00:09:30.579
U.S.D.C. And you save the record.

00:09:30.579 --> 00:09:33.034
Do those five things, and this lesson is done.

00:09:34.334 --> 00:09:37.379
This is education, not financial advice.

00:09:37.379 --> 00:09:40.324
Fees, spreads and prices change constantly.

00:09:40.324 --> 00:09:42.001
Check your own exchange's fee

00:09:42.001 --> 00:09:43.754
page for the real numbers.

00:09:43.754 --> 00:09:46.658
And nobody from this program will ever ask for

00:09:46.658 --> 00:09:49.685
your seed phrase, private keys or account access.

00:09:50.984 --> 00:09:53.949
That's buying your first crypto without overpaying.

00:09:53.949 --> 00:09:56.959
Same coin, same exchange, a smarter button.

00:09:56.959 --> 00:10:00.240
Next up, Lesson 0.4: exchange account versus

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:02.864
your own wallet, who holds the keys.
